    "created_at": "2017-01-20T17:32:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-01-20T18:04:53Z",
    "body": "@tkent-google\r\n> Proposal: `<input type=datetime-local>` accepts only valid normalized local date and time string instead of local date and time string.\r\n\r\nAFAICT based on Chrome and Edge, `<input type=datetime-local>` provides a _masked_ input where the user does _not_ deal with direct input of date as text, so it’s _somewhat_ fine for its _internal_ date format to be machine-readable and not human-readable, though I’m not sure this really makes much sense given that the resulting datetime is then anyway sent to server as _text_ that then could be output _as is_ on a webpage, while the proposed limitation would require further server-side reformatting that could probably be avoided.\r\n\r\nGiven that `<input type=datetime-local>` is not widely supported yet, it may make sense to change its datetime representation to use space instead of `T`, and change the format solely for `<input type=datetime-local>` without affecting the global datetime format in any way.\r\n\r\n@zcorpan\r\n> the space is allowed to make it a bit more human-readable when using time text content\r\n\r\nExactly. The point of allowing space inside the `TIME` element was to allow the date to be human- and machine-readable _at the same time_ and therefore to prevent unreasonable _duplication_ of the entire date, just with different date/time separators like this:\r\n\r\n    <time datetime=\"2017-01-20T18:09\">2017-01-20 18:09</time>\r\n\r\n> but maybe it could be required to use T there as well, and use datetime attribute if one wants the text content to use a different format...\r\n\r\nI believe this is a bad idea. Changing the _global_ datetime format now for the purpose of some theoretical consistency between the `TIME` element and internal `<input type=datetime-local>` representation at the cost of human convenience and web compatibility would be an unreasonable and harmful mistake.\r\n\r\nFwiw, I’m who has originally [proposed](https://www.w3.org/wiki/User:Mtanalin/time_element#h-simplify-datetime) space as allowed date/time separator in the `TIME` element and one of those who actively use the `TIME` element with no attributes for years.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
